#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527980 :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+2t)^{2}-2(2t) \\\\  & =1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527989 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+3t,\\,y=1+\\sqrt{2t},\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=2$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{2}+3(2)=10, \\\\ y & =1+\\sqrt{2(2)}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(10,3).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}}{2t+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}(2t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}(2\\cdot 2+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,3)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{14}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(0-10) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})$ is the $y$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527992 :question/problem "![](images/88/88da0f1ddf1f605186c9d921a9eade24b5f0aa9de2ae4afdc51a921586093b16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,3)$ to the point $(0,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3-3t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,3)$ and $(0,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(0-(-2))),\\,(3+t(0-3))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t-2)^{2}(3-3t) \\\\  & =4(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3(1-t) \\\\  & =-12(t-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-12(t-1)^{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527993 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{3}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=2 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =2x-4 \\\\ y & =2x-4-2 \\\\ y & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)-6 \\\\ y & =0-6 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528000 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t,\\,y=4{t}^{2}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-12t+12,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12t+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{(t-2)}^{2} & =0\\,⟹\\,t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =8(2)-3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528013 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=4, \\\\ y & =2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=2e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(4,2e).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{4}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=e} & =\\frac{e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+e}{2}=e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2e)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2e & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\\right)(x-4) \\\\ y-2e & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528079 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(u - 5\\right)^{2} \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\left(u - 5\\right)^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 u - 10 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 u - 10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u - 10)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u - 10)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(u - 5\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(u - 5\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $u - 5=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528093 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ \\left(v + 3\\right)^{2} \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=\\left(v + 3\\right)^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2 v + 6 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 v + 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 v + 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(2 v + 6\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(v + 3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(v + 3\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $v + 3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528094 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(u + 4\\right)^{2} \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\left(u + 4\\right)^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 u + 8 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 8)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 8)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(u + 4\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(u + 4\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $u + 4=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528105 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ \\left(w - 3\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=\\left(w - 3\\right)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =2 w - 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 w - 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 w - 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(2 w - 6)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(w - 3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(w - 3\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $w - 3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528109 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 3 v - 2 w \\\\ - u^{2} + v \\\\ 4 u^{2} + 2 u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u - 3 v - 2 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=- u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=4 u^{2} + 2 u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-3 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 2 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =8 u + 2 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ - 2 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 u + 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(3)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 0 \\\\ 8 u + 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 1 \\\\ 8 u + 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(3)(- 2 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(8 u + 2))+(-2)(- 2 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(8 u + 2)) \\\\  & =5 \\left(2 u + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 5 \\left(2 u + 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $10 u + 5=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528110 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + 4 v - w \\\\ - u^{2} + v \\\\ - u^{2} - 3 u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u + 4 v - w,\\,y(u,v,w)=- u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=- u^{2} - 3 u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 2 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =- 2 u - 3 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ - 2 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ - 2 u - 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 0 \\\\ - 2 u - 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 1 \\\\ - 2 u - 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(-4)(- 2 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(- 2 u - 3))+(-1)(- 2 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(- 2 u - 3)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(3 u - 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(3 u - 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $6 u - 2=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528126 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 4 v - 2 w \\\\ - 4 u^{2} + v \\\\ u^{2} - u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u - 4 v - 2 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=- 4 u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u^{2} - u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-4 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 8 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 u - 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & -2 \\\\ - 8 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 u - 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(4)\\begin{vmatrix}- 8 u & 0 \\\\ 2 u - 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 8 u & 1 \\\\ 2 u - 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(4)(- 8 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(2 u - 1))+(-2)(- 8 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(2 u - 1)) \\\\  & =- 28 u - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ - 28 u - 1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $- 28 u - 1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528128 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 2 v + 3 w \\\\ u^{2} + v \\\\ - 4 u^{2} + u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u - 2 v + 3 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=- 4 u^{2} + u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 - 8 u & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 - 8 u & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 u & 0 \\\\ 1 - 8 u & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(3)\\begin{vmatrix}2 u & 1 \\\\ 1 - 8 u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(2)(2 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(1 - 8 u))+(3)(2 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(1 - 8 u)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(14 u - 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(14 u - 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $28 u - 2=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528131 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + v - 4 w \\\\ 3 u^{2} + v \\\\ 2 u^{2} - u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u + v - 4 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=3 u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=2 u^{2} - u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =6 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =4 u - 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 6 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 u - 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}6 u & 0 \\\\ 4 u - 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\begin{vmatrix}6 u & 1 \\\\ 4 u - 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(-1)(6 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(4 u - 1))+(-4)(6 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(4 u - 1)) \\\\  & =10 u - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 10 u - 3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $10 u - 3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528132 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\left(u + 1\\right)^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 u + 2 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 2)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(u + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(u + 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $u + 1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528147 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8a71c2cbb351253304bca516db5b4b7ca7ed745264083f9eb2bbdb918e19cc0.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The angles of a triangle sum to $180^{\\circ}$. Thus the included angle is\n\n$$\nm∠B=180^{\\circ}-73^{\\circ}-73^{\\circ}=34^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=9.28, \\\\ a & =BC=9.28, \\\\ m∠B & ={34}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{34}^{∘}=0.559\\,193,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9.28\\cdot 9.28\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{34}^{∘} \\\\  & =43.0592\\cdot (0.559\\,193) \\\\  & ≈24.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528157 :question/problem "![](images/45/4507bd5611cdf0324c470755c19274b04b9572b9c416e57b319aa444fce41e8b.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The angles of a triangle sum to $180^{\\circ}$. Thus the included angle is\n\n$$\nm∠B=180^{\\circ}-46^{\\circ}-46^{\\circ}=88^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=27.26, \\\\ a & =BC=27.26, \\\\ m∠B & ={88}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{88}^{∘}=0.999\\,391,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 27.26\\cdot 27.26\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{88}^{∘} \\\\  & =371.5538\\cdot (0.999\\,391) \\\\  & ≈371.33,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528162 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4d346c76ff55f7e183aefa579c49b54022e75ea4b723c5d74954c5257dcdf2f.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=50, \\\\ b & =AC=45, \\\\ m∠A & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘}=0.819\\,152,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 45\\cdot 50\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =1125\\cdot (0.819\\,152…) \\\\  & ≈921.5\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest tenth**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528169 :question/problem "![](images/64/640d4aa7aef79eec2ea5f9a2647ce10edb80eb7a2c15c468b9302d590f5d1c11.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=25.5, \\\\ b & =AC=25.7, \\\\ m∠A & ={94}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{94}^{∘}=0.997\\,564,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 25.7\\cdot 25.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{94}^{∘} \\\\  & =327.675\\cdot (0.997\\,564) \\\\  & ≈326.88,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528172 :question/problem "![](images/06/06b277f2e975f2dce1c5a3ac20ac2f5f64aba036855501b3ce07fca3a05e7387.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The angles of a triangle sum to $180^{\\circ}$. Thus the included angle is\n\n$$\nm∠B=180^{\\circ}-71^{\\circ}-71^{\\circ}=38^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=24.03, \\\\ a & =BC=24.03, \\\\ m∠B & ={38}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{38}^{∘}=0.615\\,661,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 24.03\\cdot 24.03\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{38}^{∘} \\\\  & =288.72045\\cdot (0.615\\,661) \\\\  & ≈177.75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528186 :question/problem "![](images/48/48786aeb51beaa57e9417ba5f2ec97f7c5b9c56c26523d42408d04ec6f5688b3.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=11.5, \\\\ b & =AC=22.3, \\\\ m∠A & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘}=0.848\\,048,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 22.3\\cdot 11.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘} \\\\  & =128.225\\cdot (0.848\\,048) \\\\  & ≈108.74,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528187 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f19be54313a6cc165ea4de47a3e321a83bc90a13362bf03b6760564c04b75e1.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=23, \\\\ a & =BC=47, \\\\ m∠B & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘}=0.848\\,048,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 47\\cdot 23\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘} \\\\  & =540.5\\cdot (0.848\\,048) \\\\  & ≈458.37,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528197 :question/problem "![](images/30/30627fc9abd6a5c52ec153342ede99b7066d32c401bde1da65413f89ea7955c4.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The angles of a triangle sum to $180^{\\circ}$. Thus the included angle is\n\n$$\nm∠B=180^{\\circ}-77^{\\circ}-77^{\\circ}=26^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=16.18, \\\\ a & =BC=16.18, \\\\ m∠B & ={26}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{26}^{∘}=0.438\\,371,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16.18\\cdot 16.18\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{26}^{∘} \\\\  & =130.8962\\cdot (0.438\\,371) \\\\  & ≈57.38,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528202 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2f46ef29f428b654f06e042ce077aadcf763b92d1455411916bd3605c05b66d.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=10.9, \\\\ b & =AC=22.4, \\\\ m∠A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=0.866\\,025,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 22.4\\cdot 10.9\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =122.08\\cdot (0.866\\,025) \\\\  & ≈105.72,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528204 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa431904ff90659ad19f07a00fc1b7e8b13b5df0c4b975e73e6b2e8954c68709.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=29.9, \\\\ b & =AC=23.7, \\\\ m∠A & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘}=0.819\\,152,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 23.7\\cdot 29.9\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =354.315\\cdot (0.819\\,152) \\\\  & ≈290.24,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528213 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5ebff590d08903c465b3a5bb1359b6695f249ca61131332af899d0609f11ba8.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=22.8, \\\\ b & =AC=15.7, \\\\ m∠A & ={64}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{64}^{∘}=0.898\\,794,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 15.7\\cdot 22.8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{64}^{∘} \\\\  & =178.98\\cdot (0.898\\,794) \\\\  & ≈160.87,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528214 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0740a227de0e72a080f8f323bb49b5d7ef0ac2f838217469f2e23c81049f628.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The angles of a triangle sum to $180^{\\circ}$. Thus the included angle is\n\n$$\nm∠B=180^{\\circ}-33^{\\circ}-33^{\\circ}=114^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=13.86, \\\\ a & =BC=13.86, \\\\ m∠B & ={114}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{114}^{∘}=0.913\\,545,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 13.86\\cdot 13.86\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{114}^{∘} \\\\  & =96.0498\\cdot (0.913\\,545) \\\\  & ≈87.75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528230 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $0.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-5+0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=- \\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528231 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $- \\frac{4}{3}$ and a maximum value of $\\frac{73}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{73}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{-8}{6}+\\frac{73}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{65}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=\\frac{65}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528234 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $- \\frac{28}{3}$ and a maximum value of $\\frac{71}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{- \\frac{28}{3}+\\frac{71}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{-56}{6}+\\frac{71}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=\\frac{5}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528248 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $- \\frac{89}{6}$ and a maximum value of $\\frac{19}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{- \\frac{89}{6}+\\frac{19}{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{-89}{6}+\\frac{57}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{- \\frac{16}{3}}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=- \\frac{8}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528249 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $4.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-5+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=- \\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528260 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $0$ and a maximum value of $3.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{0+3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528261 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $\\frac{19}{3}$ and a maximum value of $\\frac{41}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{\\frac{19}{3}+\\frac{41}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{38}{6}+\\frac{41}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{79}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=\\frac{79}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528269 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-1$ and a maximum value of $16.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-1+16}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528273 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-14$ and a maximum value of $-5.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-14+(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-19}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=- \\frac{19}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528284 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $- \\frac{83}{6}$ and a maximum value of $- \\frac{22}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{- \\frac{83}{6}+\\left(- \\frac{22}{3}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{-83}{6}+\\left(\\frac{-44}{6}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{- \\frac{127}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{127}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=- \\frac{127}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529209 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $det(Q)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529214 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{4})+(- \\sqrt{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} \\\\  & =1- \\frac{7}{10}- \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{13}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=- \\frac{13}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$R$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}]}
